blish javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory rather than a same-named API type.","Identify the getInstance overload and trace the algorithm argument expression/value flow that produced the reported value.","If initialization compliance is evaluated, link the init invocation to the same factory instance and establish the required generatedKeyStore or generatedManagerFactoryParameters predicate evidence."],"runtime":["Identify the target Android API level or JVM environment, installed security providers, provider ordering, and actual algorithm string at runtime.","Establish that getInstance and init complete successfully for the selected provider and supplied trust material.","Establish whether returned TrustManager objects are installed into an SSLContext or another TLS stack and exercised during a connection."],"compatibility":["Use provider documentation or runtime tests to establish support for the selected algorithm on the target platform.","Do not treat SunX509 as Android-supported without platform-specific compatibility evidence; the supplied Android reference context identifies PKIX as the provided TrustManagerFactory algorithm.","Match the detector's rule-set release or commit to the analyzed build because a repository-head rule may differ from the bundled rules."]},"known_false_positive_shapes":["Value-flow imprecision: the runtime algorithm is PKIX or SunX509 but static analysis cannot resolve it.","A runtime-selected default or configuration-derived algorithm resolves to an accepted value but is opaque to the detector.","A provider-specific algorithm is valid for the deployed provider but intentionally excluded by the two-value CrySL allowlist.","A source substring appears to match getInstance or init but the resolved receiver is not javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory; type resolution is required to exclude namesakes.","A platform mismatch: the rule admits SunX509 but the target Android provider does not implement that name."],"authoritative_sources":["https://r