ManagerFactory.html","Reviewed Oracle’s Java SE 25 JSSE Reference Guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html","Reviewed the Java SE 25 standard TrustManagerFactory algorithm names: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html#trustmanagerfactory-algorithms","Reviewed Oracle JDK 25 provider documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/oracle-providers.html","Reviewed the Java SE 25 `KeyStore` API documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyStore.html","Reviewed the supplied upstream CrySL rule source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/TrustManagerFactory.crysl","Reviewed the official Java `javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory` API documentation for `getDefaultAlgorithm()` semantics.","Reviewed the official Oracle JSSE Reference Guide for the documented `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` default.","Reviewed the official Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification for `TrustManagerFactory` algorithm-name requirements.","Did not search for additional interoperability standards because the reported names and message do not identify one."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the governed type, including its purpose, initialization methods, null `KeyStore` parameter documentation, initialization state, support history, and required PKIX support.","limitations":"Current Java SE 25 documentation. It does not define detector predicate semantics or guarantee SunJSSE-specific behavior for all providers.","claim":"`TrustManagerFactory.init(KeyStore)` permits null; the factory creates trust managers and must be initialized before `getTrustManagers()`; PKIX is required by Java-platform implementations in this release.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html","evidence_id":"WEB-001"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle JSSE Reference Guide for SunJSSE behavior when a null keystore is supplied and for provider-dependent initialization guidance.","limitations":"Documents SunJSSE/reference implementation behavior; it is not a portable contract for every JSSE provider or non-JDK runtime.","claim":"For SunJSSE PKIX and SunX509, a null keystore triggers documented default truststore discovery; provider documentation should be consulted for the appropriate initialization method.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html","evidence_id":"WEB-002"},{"relevance":"Official standard algorithm-name specification for TrustManagerFactory algorithms.","limitations":"Describes standard Java SE algorithm support and semantics, not third-party provider behavior or detector behavior.","claim":"PKIX creates PKIX-validating X509ExtendedTrustManager objects and supports CertPathTrustManagerParameters initialization.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html#trustmanagerfactory-algorithms","evidence_id":"WEB-003"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle provider documentation for SunX509 status, SunJSSE algorithm capabilities, and provider-selection guidance.","limitations":"Oracle JDK/SunJSSE-specific documentation; it does not provide a general migration procedure or cross-provider equivalence guarantee.","claim":"SunX509 is retained for backward compatibility and should no longer be used; SunJSSE PKIX and SunX509 have different documented initialization capabilities.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/oracle-providers.html","evidence_id":"WEB-004"},{"relevance":"Official KeyStore API documentation relevant to the general loaded-before-access statement and persistence-format limitations.","limitations":"Does not prescribe a remediation for `TrustManagerFactory.init(null)` and does not define the CrySL `generatedKeyStore` predicate.","claim":"A keystore generally must be loaded before access unless loaded during construction; persistence mechanisms and formats are unspecified.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyStore.html","evidence_id":"WEB-005"},{"relevance":"Primary upstream source for the cited CrySL specification, including its governed type, event patterns, ordering, algorithm constraint, requirements, and ensures clauses.","limitations":"The mutable `refs/heads/master` URL does not verify the detector’s exact compiled artifact, revision, SHA-256, predicate semantics, or type-resolution implementation.","claim":"The rule is scoped to `javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`, declares the quoted event/order patterns, constrains the algorithm to PKIX or SunX509, and does not literally mention null keystores or loading.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/TrustManagerFactory.crysl","evidence_id":"WEB-006"},{"relevance":"Defines `TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm()` and specifies that it obtains its result from the `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` security property, with an implementation-specific result if the property is unset.","limitations":"This API documentation does not establish the target runtime's current security-property value.","claim":"The exact runtime result is property-configured rather than an unconditional literal.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html","evidence_id":"WEB-007"},{"relevance":"Documents the Oracle/OpenJDK JSSE reference default for the trust-manager-factory algorithm property.","limitations":"This is a reference-implementation default and does not establish that the analyzed runtime uses an unmodified Oracle/OpenJDK security-property configuration.","claim":"The documented Oracle/OpenJDK JSSE default value of `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` is `PKIX`.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/27/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html","evidence_id":"WEB-008"},{"relevance":"Lists Java Security Standard Algorithm Names and the required `TrustManagerFactory` algorithm support.","limitations":"Required provider support for `PKIX` does not determine the configured result of `getDefaultAlgorithm()`.","claim":"`PKIX` is a standard `TrustManagerFactory` algorithm name and is required to be supported by Java SE implementations.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html","evidence_id":"WEB-009"}],"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory` is a JSSE engine class for producing `TrustManager` objects. The API provides `getInstance` overloads to select an algorithm (and optionally a provider), `init(KeyStore)` and `init(ManagerFactoryParameters)` initialization methods, and `getTrustManagers()`. Calling `getTrustManagers()` before the factory is initialized throws `IllegalStateException`.","The reported wording refers to the documented `TrustManagerFactory.init(KeyStore ks)` event with `ks == null`. The Java API explicitly permits this parameter value: the `ks` parameter is documented as “the key store, or null.” Null is therefore not, by itself, an API-contract violation.","For SunJSSE `PKIX` and `SunX509` implementations, Oracle’s JSSE Reference Guide documents that `init(null)` causes default trust-material discovery. The documented search first considers `javax.net.ssl.trustStore`, then `<java-home>/lib/security/jssecacerts`, then `<java-home>/lib/security/cacerts`; specified absent or inaccessible stores can lead to an empty keystore/default trust manager. This is documented SunJSSE-provider behavior, not a portable behavior guaranteed by the `TrustManagerFactory` API for every provider.","The phrase “whose loading was not observed” is not stated by the `TrustManagerFactory` Java API. The `KeyStore` API generally states that a keystore must be loaded before it can be accessed, unless it was loaded during construction. That general statement does not say that `TrustManagerFactory.init(null)` requires an application to call `KeyStore.load`.","The reviewed CrySL source does not contain `null`, `trustStore`, `cacerts`, `jssecacerts`, or `load`. It declares `generatedKeyStore[keyStore]` and `generatedManagerFactoryParameters[params]` as requirements, but does not define those predicate meanings. Consequently, the rule source itself does not establish that the detector message represents a missing `KeyStore.load(...)` event.","Documented use is to obtain a factory with a supported trust-manager algorithm, initialize it using the form appropriate to the selected provider (`KeyStore` or `ManagerFactoryParameters`), and obtain trust managers after initialization. Oracle’s JSSE guide directs users to consult the provider vendor when it is unclear which initialization method is appropriate.","The Java SE 25 API states that every Java-platform implementation must support the standard `PKIX` `TrustManagerFactory` algorithm. The standard-name documentation describes `PKIX` as producing `X509ExtendedTrustManager` objects that validate certificate chains under PKIX rules and requiring support for `CertPathTrustManagerParameters` initialization.","The reviewed official sources do not describe a null `KeyStore` as misuse and do not prescribe replacing `init(null)` with an explicit keystore-loading sequence. If an application elects to provide explicit trust material, the documented API mechanism is an initialized `KeyStore`; this is documented API usage, not an official remediation mandated merely by use of null.","Oracle’s JDK 25 provider documentation describes SunJSSE `SunX509` as retained for backward compatibility and says it “should no longer be used.” It documents that SunJSSE `PKIX` supports initialization using either a `KeyStore` or `CertPathTrustManagerParameters`, while `SunX509` supports a `KeyStore` but not `ManagerFactoryParameters`. The reviewed material does not provide a step-by-step migration or equivalence guarantee.","In general-purpose applications, Oracle’s provider documentation recommends requesting cryptographic services without naming a provider, because a named provider may not be present on other Java implementations and prevents use of a higher-preference implementation. This guidance does not establish behavior for a particular provider or runtime.","`TrustManagerFactory` itself defines no persisted truststore format. The `KeyStore` documentation says whether keystores are persistent, and their persistence mechanisms, are unspecified; multiple persistence mechanisms and formats may exist. The reviewed official sources provide no truststore-format conversion or automatic migration procedure for changes to the factory algorithm or initialization mode.","The reviewed `TrustManagerFactory` sources do not define TLS wire formats, enabled protocols, or negotiated protocols. `SSLContext` is the separate JSSE API representing a secure-socket protocol implementation. No reviewed source says that changing `TrustManagerFactory` initialization changes TLS protocol negotiation.","`TrustManagerFactory` has existed since Java 1.4. In the reviewed Java SE 25 documentation, `PKIX` is required; availability of other algorithm names is provider- and implementation-dependent.","The CrySL source’s governing type declaration is, verbatim, `SPEC javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`. Its event and order clauses are, verbatim: `EVENTS\n\tg1: getInstance(algorithm);\n\tg2: getInstance(algorithm, _);\n\tGet := g1 | g2;\n\n\ti1: init(keyStore);\n\ti2: init(params);\n\tInit := i1 | i2;\n\n\tgtm1: trustManager = getTrustManagers();\n\tGetTrustMng := gtm1;\n\nORDER\n\tGet, Init, GetTrustMng?`.","The CrySL source does constrain the declared parameter-list shapes: `getInstance` has either one `algorithm` argument or two arguments with `_` as the second; `init` has exactly one declared `keyStore` or `params` argument; and `getTrustManagers` has no declared arguments and is assigned to `trustManager`. The source’s literal constraint is, verbatim, `algorithm in {"PKIX", "SunX509"};`.","The CrySL source additionally states, verbatim: `REQUIRES\n\tgeneratedKeyStore[keyStore];\n\tgeneratedManagerFactoryParameters[params];\n\nENSURES\n\tgeneratedTrustManager[this] after Init;\n\tgeneratedTrustManagers[trustManager] after GetTrustMng;`.","Because the source is explicitly a `SPEC javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`, it does not state a global name-only rule for every method named `getInstance`, `init`, or `getTrustManagers`. A method merely sharing one of those names is not identified by this source itself as a match. The source does not document analyzer behavior for subtype resolution, unresolved calls, aliases, reflection, or generated code.","`TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm()` is declared by `javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`, which is among the supplied imports.","The `TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm()` API returns the default TrustManagerFactory algorithm name as specified by the `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` security property. If that property is not set, the result is implementation-specific.","Consequently, there is no environment-independent literal string to which `TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm()` always evaluates at runtime. Its exact value is the current value of `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm`, or an implementation-specific value if that property is absent.","For the Oracle/OpenJDK JSSE reference configuration, the documented default value of `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` is `PKIX`; therefore the expression evaluates to `PKIX` in that unmodified configuration.","`PKIX` is a Java Security Standard Algorithm Name for a `TrustManagerFactory`, and Java SE requires support for `PKIX`. This support requirement does not require `getDefaultAlgorithm()` to return `PKIX`, because the API selects the default through the security property.","The reported class and method names, and the detector message concerning `TrustManagerFactory.init(null)`, do not themselves identify a published wire protocol, file format, remote-service API, or external standard that requires a particular `TrustManagerFactory.getInstance(...)` algorithm-name argument."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The official API documentation does not define how the detector translates its `generatedKeyStore[keyStore]` requirement into the reported phrase “whose loading was not observed.”","The reviewed CrySL source does not reveal the detector’s exact treatment of null values, unknown values, interprocedural flow, aliases, inheritance, reflection, Kotlin/JVM desugaring, or unresolved receiver types.","The mutable upstream CrySL URL does not establish that it is byte-for-byte the artifact used by the finding, and the stated rule SHA-256 was not independently verified.","The target Java runtime, provider, and version are not identified. Therefore, whether SunJSSE’s documented null-keystore search behavior applies is unresolved.","No reviewed official source supplies a persisted-format migration procedure, wire-protocol migration procedure, or a compatibility/equivalence guarantee for moving among null/default trust material, explicit keystores, PKIX, SunX509, or provider-specific configurations.","Which concrete runtime, JDK/provider version, and effective Java security-property configuration applies? Without those details, the exact runtime value of `TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm()` cannot be established beyond the current `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` value or the implementation-specific fallback.","No external interoperability specification is identified by the reported class, method, or detector message."],"parts":[{"part":"shared"},{"part":"case"}]},"previously_requested_evidence":{"requested":4,"requests_answered_from_repository":4,"entries_supplied":4,"declined":[],"evidence_ids":["SUP-001","SUP-002","SUP-003","SUP-004"]}}
